# probe_arith_result_bound_to_a_name.gy — the arithmetic the print position answers, one statement
# earlier: the answer is bound to a name before anything asks to see it. CPython, the interpreted leg
# and the compiled leg all print these numbers, because the binding takes the same (payload, tag) pair
# road the print dispatch has taken since ADR 0265 and the name becomes a tagged binding with it
# (roadmap Gap R.138, ADR 0267).
#
# The last three lines are the other half of the same road: a binding that is *not* a pair retires the
# tag, so the name means what its latest binding says — ADR 0172's latest-binding rule carried to the
# tagged variable (roadmap Gap R.142).
xs = []
xs.append([7, 8])
n = xs[0][0] * 2
print(n)
print(xs[0][0] + 1)
print(-xs[0][0])
m = xs[0][1] - 3
print(m)
both = xs[0][0] + xs[0][1]
print(both)
ys = []
ys.append([7.5, 8])
f = ys[0][0] * 2
print(f)
d = {}
d["k"] = 40
e = d["k"] + 2
print(e)
# A plain number retires the pair…
n = 3
print(n)
# …and so does a container: print(n) stops asking the tag the arithmetic left behind.
n = [1, 2]
print(n)
n = {5, 6}
print(n)
